# What git is allowed to change about a file on its way into the object store
# and back out of it.
#
# With this file absent, that is decided by whatever core.autocrlf the
# contributor's platform set when git was installed. The bytes in a clone then
# depend on who cloned it, which is tolerable for source and is not tolerable
# here: this project's fixtures are documents that are meant to be wrong in one
# specific way, and for several of them the defining byte is exactly the byte a
# normalisation setting removes. A budget file that is invalid because of a
# stray carriage return, checked in raw and checked out somewhere with the
# other setting, arrives at the validator clean. The validator correctly
# accepts it and the suite goes green while proving the opposite of what it
# claims.
#
# So the treatment is fixed here rather than inherited. Everything in this tree
# is text, is stored with LF endings, and is checked out with LF endings on
# every platform. eol=lf rather than eol=native, because native means two
# clones of one commit hold different bytes and every claim about a file's
# content has to name which platform it was made on.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The types this tree holds, named rather than left to the content guess in
# text=auto. The guess is right almost always, and the case it gets wrong is a
# file that happens to contain a byte it reads as binary, which is the shape a
# fixture is most likely to have.

*.py            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.lock          text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
DCO             text eol=lf

# There is deliberately no rule marking a fixture directory binary. That would
# be the other way of solving this, and it fails quietly: a rule attached to a
# path protects the fixtures somebody remembered to put there, and says nothing
# about the one written next to the test that uses it. The convention this
# repository takes instead is that a fixture whose content matters at the byte
# level is carried encoded in source, where no setting anywhere can rewrite it,
# and the encoding is a helper with one job so that skipping it is visible in
# review. tests/fixtures.py is that helper and says the rest.
